    "created_at": "2026-05-11T09:54:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-05-11T09:54:12Z",
    "body": "Thanks for your feedback, @annevk,\n\n> I'm not sure this is generally true. Reportedly WebKit at least has some caching here\n\nI re-checked WebKit's implementation of CodeCache (e.g., [here](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/JavaScriptCore/runtime/CodeCache.h)), and it turned out that WebKit has already implemented disk-persistent code cache for inline scripts (Correct me if I'm wrong; I'm not a WebKit expert.) Still, we believe the cachehint attribute remains valuable as a way for developers to explicitly tell browsers to manage cache eviction and storage more efficiently than relying solely on heuristics.\n\n> This proposal is very light on evidence\n\nRegarding evidence, it is hard to measure the impacts for the whole Web platform from the public data because metrics around script execution are not exposed to Web API, while we expect solid performance gains based on Chrome-internal metrics. We would like to run A/B tests through an origin trial for this attribute to gather concrete performance gains.\n\n> Although the proposed name has \"hint\" in the name, the explainer at least seems to make normative requirements about behavior\n\nOn naming, we are open to alternatives for both the cachehint attribute and the \"never\" value. For the former, we could change the name for a more normative name like cachecontrol. For the latter, we could consider a name that discourages caching for one-off scripts (like \"unwilling\" or \"ephemeral\") rather than strictly prohibiting it. We’d appreciate your thoughts on what naming would best align with the HTML spec.\n@hiroshige-g Do you have any thoughts on the naming?",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
