    "created_at": "2026-06-01T13:47:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-06-01T13:49:54Z",
    "body": "I'm curious as to more of the motivation for this change. Why are we pointing towards using inline scripts? Is this to save on network roundtrips? Does h2 push or h3 obviate the need for such in-lining? What about other knobs such as speculation rules? What would usage for developers look like for this, given most \"large\" websites use a bundler and split code into \"chunks\". Is the need for this obviated by other techniques such as being able to populate the module graph, e.g. something like https://github.com/tc39/proposal-module-declarations?\n\nThis feels a bit like the \"critical CSS\" pattern. I second @annevk's comments that I'd like to see some more work on the problem statement, and more examples as to the motivating use cases for this.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
